for(s=0;s<7;++s){o=B.EO[s]
r+=B.b.D(A.a([o.a,o.b,o.c],p),j)+"\n"}r=r+"The US 902-928 MHz band is the widest and most permissive (full 1-16 MHz channels). Europe restricts to the narrow 1/2 MHz channels. The Wi-Fi Alliance is advocating for globally harmonized access at 915-925 MHz.\n\nChannel widths\n"+(B.b.D(A.a(["Width","Use","Region availability"],p),j)+"\n")
for(s=0;s<5;++s){n=B.FV[s]
r+=B.b.D(A.a([n.a,n.b,n.c],p),j)+"\n"}r+="Even the widest HaLow channel (16 MHz) is narrower than the minimum 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi channel (20 MHz). Narrow channels concentrate energy, which is how HaLow reaches farther.\n\nHeadline numbers\n"
for(s=0;s<4;++s){m=B.EK[s]
r+=m.a+": "+m.b+". "+m.c+"\n"}r=r+"The defensible headline is 86.7 Mbps single stream, in the Wi-Fi Alliance source. Wikipedia cites 433.3 Mbps for a 4-spatial-stream maximum, but a 4x scaling of the WFA figure gives about 347 Mbps, and first-generation HaLow silicon is single-stream. Read 86.7 Mbps as the number a field tech will see ceilinged.\n\nSingle-stream data rates (Mbps, LGI / SGI)\n"+(B.b.D(A.a(["MCS","Modulation","1 MHz","2 MHz","4 MHz","8 MHz","16 MHz"],p),j)+"\n")
for(s=0;s<11;++s){l=B.F7[s]
r+=B.b.D(A.a(["MCS "+l.a,l.b,l.c,l.d,l.e,l.f,l.r],p),j)+"\n"}r+="\nPower efficiency\n"
for(s=0;s<5;++s){q=B.F6[s]
r+=q.a+"\t"+q.b+"\n"}r+="\nPHY / MAC\n"
for(s=0;s<6;++s){q=B.E0[s]
r+=q.a+"\t"+q.b+"\n"}r+="The clean mental model: the 802.11ac PHY clocked at one tenth. Same OFDM machinery, ten times slower clock, so symbols are 10x longer (more robust over distance and multipath) and rates are about a tenth of 802.11ac.\n\nUse cases\n"
for(s=0;s<7;++s)r+="- "+B.In[s]+"\n"
r=r+"\nVs other IoT radios\n"+(B.b.D(A.a(["Tech","Band","Data rate","Range","IP-native"],p),j)+"\n")
for(s=0;s<7;++s){k=B.Et[s]
r+=B.b.D(A.a([k.a,k.b,k.c,k.d,k.e],p),j)+"\n"}r+="HaLow sits in the middle of the map: more range and device count than BLE, Zigbee, and Z-Wave, and more data rate plus native IP than LoRaWAN, Sigfox, and NB-IoT. It does not match LoRa/Sigfox/NB-IoT for multi-kilometer range, and unlike carrier NB-IoT it needs its own AP infrastructure. Its strongest case is replacing short-range mesh radios with longer reach and direct IP, and carrying video.\n\nMaturity (2026)\n"
for(s=0;s<4;++s){q=B.IH[s]
r+=q.a+"\t"+q.b+"\n"}r+="HaLow is real, certified, and shipping with a clear silicon leader and sub-$130 developer gateways, but it is at the early-adoption stage in 2026. It has not displaced Zigbee or Z-Wave yet; that is a roadmap claim, not a 2026 fact.\n"
