B.chs=new A.rI("microwave-oven","Microwave oven","2.4 GHz","A broad, wandering blob that climbs into the upper 2.4 GHz channels (worst around channel 9 to 11), pulsing on and off with the mains cycle. It is never centered on channel 6.")
B.chv=new A.rI("bluetooth-classic","Bluetooth Classic","2.4 GHz","A dense, fine speckle of thin 1 MHz hops scattered across the whole band every frame: 79 channels, hopping about 1,600 times per second, with no fixed home.")
B.chu=new A.rI("analog-video-camera","Analog video camera","2.4 GHz","Three adjacent continuous carriers on a fixed frequency, the video carrier flanked by its audio and color subcarriers. It is steady, never hops, and never stops.")
B.cht=new A.rI("ble","Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE)","2.4 GHz","Three strong, persistent pickets on the advertising channels 37, 38, and 39 (2402, 2426, and 2480 MHz), parked in the gaps around Wi-Fi 1, 6, and 11, with faint data-hop speckle behind them.")
B.chw=new A.rI("baby-monitor","Baby monitor (analog)","2.4 GHz","Often a single continuous fixed carrier like an analog camera, but it varies by model: some hop, and many newer units are DECT at 1.9 GHz, which is harmless to Wi-Fi. Read the signature, do not assume.")
B.chp=new A.rI("drone-fpv-downlink","Drone / FPV downlink","5 GHz / 5.8 GHz","A wide, ragged, frequency-agile block up at 5.8 GHz that jogs over time. Analog FPV is a continuous carrier; digital systems spread a wider, adaptive OFDM block. Confidence is lower here and the shape is model-dependent.")
B.chr=new A.rI("zigbee-802-15-4","ZigBee / 802.15.4","2.4 GHz","A narrow (about 2 MHz) fixed picket, bursty and low duty cycle, sitting on a channel chosen to dodge Wi-Fi 1, 6, and 11. Its position is steady; it does not hop.")
B.chx=new A.rI("cordless-phone-24ghz","Analog cordless phone","2.4 GHz","A strong carrier that steps slowly across a few channels, leaving a break in the waterfall at each change. The 2.4 GHz analog kind interferes; DECT 6.0 at 1.9 GHz does not.")
B.chq=new A.rI("wireless-bridge","Continuous wireless bridge","2.4 GHz","A constant, wide (about 20 MHz) block at close to 100 percent duty cycle. It is among the most damaging interferers because it never yields the channel.")
