B.aiX=new A.jA("Allowed characters","Letters (A-Z, a-z), digits (0-9), and the hyphen (-). No underscores, spaces, or other symbols in a standard hostname.","RFC 952; RFC 1123 \xa72.1")
B.aiM=new A.jA("First character","May be a letter OR a digit. (RFC 952 required a letter; RFC 1123 \xa72.1 relaxed this to allow a leading digit.)","RFC 1123 \xa72.1")
B.aiU=new A.jA("Last character","Must be a letter or digit. A label may NOT end with a hyphen.","RFC 952")
B.aiN=new A.jA("Hyphen placement","Interior only. No leading hyphen, no trailing hyphen.","RFC 952")
B.aiT=new A.jA("Case sensitivity","Case-insensitive (comparisons ignore case).","RFC 952; RFC 1035 \xa72.3.3")
B.aiK=new A.jA("Max label length","63 octets per label (single component between dots).","RFC 1035 \xa72.3.4 / \xa73.1")
B.aiL=new A.jA("Max FQDN length","253 characters (255 octets on the wire). The 255-octet wire-format cap, after length-prefix and root-label overhead, yields a 253-character practical text limit.","RFC 1035 \xa72.3.4 / \xa73.1")
B.aiP=new A.jA("Not all-numeric","A valid host name can never have the dotted-decimal form #.#.#.#. at least the top-level component must be alphabetic, so a hostname is never ambiguous with an IPv4 literal.","RFC 1123 \xa72.1")
B.H0=s([B.aiX,B.aiM,B.aiU,B.aiN,B.aiT,B.aiK,B.aiL,B.aiP],t.QA)
B.H1=s(['Matter is not a radio. It is a Connectivity Standards Alliance application layer that runs over Thread, Wi-Fi, and Ethernet, and is commissioned over BLE. It has no PHY of its own. When someone asks whether a device is "Matter or Wi-Fi," the honest answer is that Matter can ride Wi-Fi.','802.15.4 is not 2.4 GHz only. The standard also defines 868 MHz (EU) and 902 to 928 MHz (US) sub-GHz PHYs, and Zigbee has sub-GHz profiles. Nearly all modern Zigbee, Thread, and Matter gear ships 2.4 GHz, which is why the coexistence problem is real, but "2.4 only" is wrong.',"CBRS and private 5G do not interfere with Wi-Fi. CBRS lives at 3.55 to 3.70 GHz, which does not overlap any Wi-Fi band. The conflict is over the job and the budget, not the spectrum: private cellular competes with Wi-Fi for the enterprise-connectivity use case, and vendors pitch it against you. It never competes for your air."],t.s)
