B.cen=new A.rF("A spectrum analyzer reveals the non-Wi-Fi interferers a Wi-Fi adapter is structurally blind to. That difference is the whole reason spectrum analysis exists in Wi-Fi work.",null)
B.XS=new A.aMk(null)
B.caS=new A.oD("Two different instruments",null)
B.cfL=new A.oK("A Wi-Fi NIC is a protocol decoder. It tunes to an 802.11 channel, demodulates the frames it can, and reports what it decodes: SSIDs, BSSIDs, channels in use, data rates, and retries. It only perceives energy it can interpret as 802.11. Anything else is lumped into a vague noise bucket, and many adapters do not report it at all.",null)
B.cfI=new A.oK("A spectrum analyzer measures raw RF power versus frequency, no matter the protocol or modulation. It does not care whether the energy is Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, a microwave oven, or a video camera. It shows the energy as it exists in the air.",null)
B.caY=new A.oD("The load-bearing point",null)
B.cfM=new A.oK("When users report poor performance but a Wi-Fi scanner shows a clean channel, the missing piece is almost always a non-802.11 energy source that only a spectrum analyzer can see.",null)
B.cfQ=new A.oK("A subtler distinction is worth keeping. A NIC\u2019s channel utilization tells you the airtime is busy. A spectrum analyzer, and its density / duty-cycle view, tells you how busy a given frequency is and what the energy looks like, which is what lets you identify the source.",null)
