B.cej=new A.rF("Three views answer three different questions. A real diagnosis uses all three: spot it live, fingerprint it in the waterfall, quantify it in density.",null)
B.caX=new A.oD("Live FFT: power versus frequency",null)
B.cfP=new A.oK("X is frequency, Y is amplitude in dBm, one live trace. Tall peaks are strong signals; the flat bottom is the noise floor. The width of a feature tells you its bandwidth, its position tells you its center frequency. This answers what is transmitting, where, and how strong, right now.",null)
B.caR=new A.oD("Waterfall / spectrogram: frequency, time, color",null)
B.cfR=new A.oK("One axis is frequency, the other is time (usually scrolling), and color encodes amplitude. The color-to-amplitude mapping is set by the tool\u2019s palette and scale, so always read the legend rather than assuming. A steady horizontal band is a continuous transmitter on a fixed frequency; repeating vertical streaks are periodic bursts; a signal that wanders across frequencies over time is frequency hopping. This is the view that exposes the pattern, which is how you fingerprint an interferer.",null)
B.caT=new A.oD("Density / duty cycle: how often a frequency is occupied",null)
B.cfH=new A.oK("X is frequency, Y is amplitude, and color or brightness encodes how often each point has been occupied over the accumulation window. A frequency lit brightly across a long window is occupied most of the time, a high duty cycle and a serious problem for Wi-Fi, which needs the channel clear to talk. A faint smear is occasional. This is how you prioritize: a 90 percent duty interferer matters far more than a 2 percent one even when both peak at the same amplitude.",null)
B.b1m=s([B.cej,B.m,B.caX,B.i,B.cfP,B.m,B.caR,B.i,B.cfR,B.m,B.caT,B.i,B.cfH],t.p)
B.fg=new A.a7I(1,"multi")
B.Ui=new A.U7(2,"selectable")
B.l5=new A.a0w(1,"estimate")
B.bIq=new A.k6("ookla","Ookla Speedtest","Ookla, LLC (Ziff Davis)","https://www.speedtest.net","Download, upload, idle latency, jitter, packet loss; loaded latency in newer clients.","Multi-stream TCP, scaling up to 8 connections at higher round-trip time. Samples throughput across time slices and discards outliers. Picks a nearby server by default; distant servers selectable. The current slice math is not public.",B.fg,B.Ui,"~200 MB at 100 Mbps; approaches ~1 GB on a gigabit link",B.l5,"No (proprietary; separate SDK and CLI sold)",!1,null)
B.pK=new A.U7(0,"cdnEdge")
B.bIr=new A.k6("fast-com","Fast.com","Netflix","https://fast.com",'Download headline; upload plus unloaded and loaded latency under "Show more info".',"Runs against Netflix Open Connect appliances, the same CDN that streams Netflix video. Opens several connections, varies the count by conditions, and ends the test adaptively once results are stable.",B.fg,B.pK,"Adaptive; depends on link speed and run length (no fixed figure)",B.l5,"No",!1,"Runs on Netflix's own production CDN (Open Connect), the cleanest example of measuring the CDN next door. Netflix states it is not for enterprise or third-party certification.")
B.bIw=new A.k6("cloudflare","Cloudflare Speed Test","Cloudflare","https://speed.cloudflare.com","Download, upload, idle and loaded latency, idle and loaded jitter, packet loss, plus AIM scores for streaming / gaming / video-chat.","Runs on Cloudflare Workers at the edge nearest you. Sends progressively larger payloads to mimic real loading rather than only saturating the pipe, and reports the 90th percentile, not peak. Interleaves empty requests to measure loaded latency.",B.fg,B.pK,"Up to ~200 MB (caps near there; varies with measured speed)",B.l5,"Client component open-source (cloudflare/speedtest); edge backend is Cloudflare's, not self-hostable",!1,null)
B.Vh=new A.a7I(0,"single")
B.wo=new A.U7(1,"distantServer")
B.nB=new A.a0w(2,"low")
B.bIz=new A.k6("mlab-ndt",'M-Lab NDT (Google "speed test")',"Measurement Lab (M-Lab) consortium; surfaced by Google Search","https://speed.measurementlab.net","Download, upload, minimum round-trip latency, loss rate, plus fine-grained transport metrics.","Single-stream TCP over a WebSocket, about 10 seconds each direction; current ndt7 uses TCP BBR. Single stream is a deliberate choice to diagnose path problems, and it hits a fixed M-Lab research server, not a hyper-local edge, so numbers read lower by design.",B.Vh,B.wo,"Bounded by the ~10 s x 2 transfers; scales with link, shorter than saturating tests",B.nB,"Yes (Go server; JS / Swift / Kotlin / Java clients on GitHub)",!1,"M-Lab is the measurement platform; Google merely surfaces it in search. Credit Measurement Lab (M-Lab), not Google, as the service.")
B.bIx=new A.k6("nperf","nPerf","nPerf SAS (Lyon, France)","https://www.nperf.com","Download, upload, latency, plus a browsing test (web-page load) and a streaming test (360p / 720p / 1080p video buffering).","Proprietary algorithm against a worldwide dedicated server network sized to saturate the link; server auto- or manually selected. Distinctive for combining raw speed with browse and stream quality in one run. Methodology is largely vendor-stated.",B.fg,B.Ui,"Saturating run plus a 10 s video at three resolutions; more than a speed-only test (no published figure)",B.nB,"No",!1,null)
B.bIA=new A.k6("waveform","Waveform Bufferbloat Test","Waveform","https://www.waveform.com/tools/bufferbloat","Download, upload, and the headline bufferbloat grade (A+ to F) = latency increase under load, across unloaded / downlink-saturated / uplink-saturated stages.","Adds a bufferbloat grading layer on top of Cloudflare's test backend. Measures latency during the saturated phases and compares it to idle.",B.fg,B.pK,"Comparable to a Cloudflare run (~up to ~200 MB), scales with link",B.nB,"No (front-end tool; backend is Cloudflare's)",!1,'Built on Cloudflare\'s test backend. Best example that the same bytes can be graded for latency-under-load, and that two "different" tests can share one backend.')
B.wp=new A.U7(3,"selfHosted")
B.bIv=new A.k6("librespeed","LibreSpeed","Open-source project","https://librespeed.org","Download, upload, ping (latency), jitter, packet loss; optional IP / ISP.","Lightweight vanilla JavaScript using XHR and Web Workers (no Flash, Java, or WebSocket). Multi-stream; the server generates garbage chunks on the fly and discards them.",B.fg,B.wp,"No metered cost when self-hosted (your own client to your own server); scales with link over WAN",B.nA,"Yes, LGPLv3 (PHP, Go, Rust, Node backends; Docker images), the standout self-hostable option",!1,null)
B.bIu=new A.k6("speedof-me","SpeedOf.Me","SpeedOf.Me, LLC","https://speedof.me","Download, upload, latency, jitter.","Pure HTML5 / JavaScript. Single connection. Downloads progressively larger samples (128 KB up to 128 MB) and accepts the last sample that took over 8 seconds. Positioned as closer to real single-file browsing than multi-thread tests.",B.Vh,B.wo,"Bounded by the sample ladder; up to ~128 MB on a fast link, far less on a slow one",B.l5,"No",!1,null)
B.bJo=new A.a7I(2,"sampled")
B.bIs=new A.k6("orb","Orb","Orb (from the original creators of Speedtest and Downdetector)","https://orb.net","Continuous monitoring, not a one-shot test. An Orb Score (0 to 100) built from Responsiveness, Reliability, and Speed.",'A lightweight always-on agent on hardware you own. Responsiveness is sampled twice per second; reliability tracks drops, recovery, and outage duration; speed uses a small ~10 MB file on a cadence (default hourly). It answers "how is my connection over time", catching dropouts a one-shot test misses.',B.bJo,B.wo,"Speed sampling is light (~10 MB file) but runs repeatedly; cumulative-per-day is the relevant figure, not per-test",B.l5,"Agent free-tier installable (Pi / Docker); Cloud API paid; not open-source",!0,null)
B.bIp=new A.k6("openspeedtest","OpenSpeedTest","Open-source project","https://openspeedtest.com","Download, upload, ping, jitter.","Open-source HTML5 test that needs no server-side language; runs in the browser with a Node or Docker server. A direct LibreSpeed alternative and a second self-host option for testing a LAN.",B.fg,B.wp,"No metered cost when self-hosted; scales with link over WAN",B.nA,"Yes (browser plus Node / Docker; no server-side language)",!1,null)
B.bIy=new A.k6("speedtest-cli-iperf3","Speedtest CLI / iperf3","Ookla (CLI) / open-source (iperf3)","https://iperf.fr","Command-line throughput. iperf3 measures true point-to-point throughput with no CDN in the path at all.","Not a website. Professionals run Ookla's CLI for scripted speedtests and iperf3 for point-to-point throughput between two hosts you control, which removes server choice and CDN proximity from the result entirely.",B.fg,B.wp,"You set it (duration / parallel streams); iperf3 traffic is between your own two hosts",B.nA,"iperf3 is open-source (BSD); Ookla CLI is proprietary",!1,null)
B.bIt=new A.k6("isp-branded","ISP-branded tests (Xfinity, AT&T, Google Fiber)","Individual ISPs","https://speedtest.net","Download, upload, latency on the ISP's own test page.","Most are a skinned Ookla or M-Lab / SamKnows backend. An ISP's own test measures to the ISP's own well-placed server, the friendliest possible path, so treat the result with that bias in mind.",B.fg,B.pK,"Inherits the underlying backend's cost (often Ookla, so scales with link)",B.nB,"No (the visible page; the backend is usually a third party)",!1,"Usually skinned Ookla or M-Lab / SamKnows, not the ISP's own measurement infrastructure. The test points at the ISP's own server, the friendliest path.")
